# OPTIONAL local HTTPS in front of the Organization Portal, so a phone on the same Wi-Fi can use the camera
# (browsers only allow camera access on https:// pages and on localhost). See docs/scanning.md.
#
#   1. find this computer's Wi-Fi/LAN address:   python scripts/lan_ip.py
#   2. put it in .env:                           LAN_HOST=192.168.1.20
#   3. start the proxy:                          docker compose --profile https up -d --force-recreate https-proxy
#   4. on the phone, install the local root certificate, then open  https://192.168.1.20:3443
#
# `tls internal` signs certificates with Caddy's own local certificate authority. Certificates are made ONLY for the names
# listed below (localhost, 127.0.0.1 and LAN_HOST): there is no on-demand issuing, so nobody who can reach the port can make
# the proxy create certificates for names of their choosing. Browsers send no server name when you type an IP address, so
# `default_sni` makes the LAN_HOST certificate the one served to such connections (otherwise Caddy would fall back to the
# container's internal Docker address, which is never the address the phone typed).
#
# This is for LOCAL TESTING ONLY: in production use a real certificate (your load balancer, Caddy with a public domain, ...).
{
	skip_install_trust
	default_sni {$LAN_HOST:127.0.0.1}
	auto_https disable_redirects
	servers {
		# Only TCP is published (3443/tcp), so do not advertise HTTP/3 (UDP) to clients.
		protocols h1 h2
	}
}

https://{$LAN_HOST:127.0.0.1}:3443, https://localhost:3443 {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy dashboard:3001
}
